---
/**
 * CTC, gross salary and in-hand salary, walked through with the engine's own numbers. Every table
 * is computed at build time, so it always matches the calculator.
 */
import DocLayout from '../layouts/DocLayout.astro';
import { bracketPath } from '../lib/brackets';
import { ctcData } from '../lib/explainers';
import { formatPercent, formatRupees } from '../lib/format';

const data = ctcData();
const money = (amount: number) => (amount < 0 ? `−${formatRupees(-amount)}` : formatRupees(amount));
const gross = data.steps.find((s) => s.kind === 'subtotal')!;
const inHand = data.steps.find((s) => s.kind === 'total')!;
const v = data.variable;
---

<DocLayout
  title="CTC, gross and in-hand salary: what each one means"
  description="The three salary figures on every Indian offer letter, worked through for FY 2026-27: what CTC includes, how gross salary is reached, and what is deducted before your in-hand pay."
  updated="2026-10-10"
  ogImage="ctc-vs-in-hand-salary"
  about={['Cost to company', 'Gross salary', 'In-hand salary', 'Income tax in India']}
  deva="कमाई"
>
  <p>
    An Indian offer letter can describe the same job with three different numbers. They are not
    interchangeable, and the gap between the first and the last is what surprises most people in
    their first month.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>CTC</strong>, cost to company, is everything your employer spends on you in a year,
      including money that never reaches your bank account.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Gross salary</strong> is what is paid through payroll before deductions: Basic, HRA,
      special allowance and other allowances.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>In-hand salary</strong>, also called take-home or net pay, is what is actually
      credited each month, after your provident fund, professional tax and income tax.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>From CTC to in-hand, step by step</h2>
  <p>
    A {data.exampleLpa} LPA offer with the calculator's standard structure: Basic at half of CTC, PF
    at the wage ceiling, gratuity and employer PF counted inside CTC, Karnataka professional tax and
    the new tax regime. All figures are for the year.
  </p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Step</th>
          <th scope="col">A year</th>
          <th scope="col">Where it goes</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {data.steps.map((s) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{s.kind === 'minus' ? s.label : <strong>{s.label}</strong>}</td>
            <td>{s.kind === 'minus' ? money(s.amount) : <strong>{money(s.amount)}</strong>}</td>
            <td>{s.note ?? ''}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    So a {data.exampleLpa} lakh CTC becomes {formatRupees(gross.amount)} of gross salary and{' '}
    {formatRupees(inHand.amount)} in hand, or{' '}
    <strong>{formatRupees(data.takeHomeMonthly)} a month</strong>. The employer PF and gratuity are
    still yours, but as savings you reach later, not as salary.
  </p>

  <h2>The gap at different salaries</h2>
  <p>
    The same structure at other salaries. Below about ₹12 lakh, provident fund and gratuity make up
    most of the gap; above it, income tax takes over.
  </p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">CTC</th>
          <th scope="col">Gross a month</th>
          <th scope="col">Deducted a month</th>
          <th scope="col">In hand a month</th>
          <th scope="col">Share of CTC in hand</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {data.rows.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td>
              <a href={bracketPath(r.lpa)}>{r.lpa} LPA</a>
            </td>
            <td>{formatRupees(r.grossMonthly)}</td>
            <td>{formatRupees(r.deductionsMonthly)}</td>
            <td>{formatRupees(r.takeHomeMonthly)}</td>
            <td>{formatPercent(r.share, 0)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    "Deducted" is your own PF, any ESI, professional tax and income tax. The share of CTC also
    counts the employer PF and gratuity that never pass through your payslip.
  </p>

  <h2>Variable pay is not part of your monthly salary</h2>
  <p>
    Many offers put part of the CTC into a performance bonus or variable pay. It is carved out of
    the same CTC, so it lowers the monthly figure, and it is paid once or twice a year only if
    targets are met.
  </p>
  <p>
    At {v.lpa} LPA with {v.percent}% variable pay ({formatRupees(v.variableAnnual)} a year), the
    monthly in-hand falls from {formatRupees(v.monthlyWithout)} to {formatRupees(v.monthlyWith)}. If
    the bonus is paid in full, the year's total is the same, {formatRupees(v.annualWith)}; you just
    receive {formatRupees(v.monthlyWithout - v.monthlyWith)} a month later, as a lump sum. If it is
    not paid, that money is gone. When you compare offers, compare the fixed part.
  </p>

  <h2>What else can sit inside a CTC</h2>
  <p>
    Some employers also count things the calculator does not model, such as a joining or retention
    bonus, insurance premiums, meal cards or a company car. None of them changes your monthly
    in-hand, and a one-time bonus makes the first year's CTC look larger than every year after it.
    Ask for a component-wise breakdown before you compare.
  </p>

  <h2>Comparing two offers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Compare <strong>monthly in-hand</strong> on the fixed pay, then add the variable part at the
      rate it has actually been paid.
    </li>
    <li>
      Check whether employer PF and gratuity are <strong>inside</strong> each CTC. An offer that
      pays them on top is worth more than the same CTC with them inside.
    </li>
    <li>
      Check whether PF is on the wage ceiling or on full Basic. Full-Basic PF lowers in-hand but
      saves much more; see the <a href="/provident-fund">provident fund guide</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Put both offers into the <a href="/">calculator</a> and use its share link to keep each
      result, so you can open them side by side.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The <a href="/how-it-works">method page</a> lists every rate and source behind these numbers.
  </p>
</DocLayout>
